APX.nz = {"country":{"name":"New Zealand","tagline":"One of the world's most southerly wine countries, which turned one grape and one valley into a global style in a single generation.","classification":"New Zealand's wine Geographical Indications are registered by the Intellectual Property Office of New Zealand (IPONZ) under the Geographical Indications (Wine and Spirits) Registration Act 2006, which came into force in July 2017. There are three 'enduring' GIs created by statute (New Zealand, North Island, South Island) and 19 registered wine GIs: Northland, Auckland (with Matakana, Kumeu and Waiheke Island inside it), Gisborne, Hawke's Bay (with Central Hawke's Bay), Wairarapa (with Gladstone and Martinborough), Nelson, Marlborough, Canterbury (with North Canterbury, which contains Waipara Valley), Waitaki Valley North Otago and Central Otago (with Bannockburn).\n\nA GI only defines a boundary. To use one on a label, at least 85 percent of the wine must come from the named area (the same 85 percent rule applies to variety and vintage). There are no rules on grape varieties, yields or winemaking. The registration process does require evidence that the area's wines have a quality, reputation or characteristic linked to it, which is why only established places are registered and why informal sub-areas such as Marlborough's Awatere Valley, Hawke's Bay's Gimblett Gravels or Central Otago's Bendigo are not GIs. Gimblett Gravels instead uses a trademark controlled by its winegrowers' association.\n\nOther labelling signals matter more in practice: Sustainable Winegrowing New Zealand certification (members cover about 98 percent of vineyard area), organic (BioGro) and biodynamic (Demeter) certification, and in Central Otago and Martinborough the vineyard name."},"regions":[{"name":"North Island","slug":"north-island","tagline":"The warmer island: Hawke's Bay reds, Kumeu Chardonnay, Martinborough Pinot and the vineyards where New Zealand wine began.","ids":["nz-auckland","nz-central-hawkes-bay","nz-gisborne","nz-gladstone","nz-hawkes-bay","nz-kumeu","nz-martinborough","nz-matakana","nz-north-island","nz-northland","nz-waiheke-island","nz-wairarapa"]},{"name":"South Island","slug":"south-island","tagline":"Four fifths of New Zealand's vines, from the Sauvignon flats of Marlborough to the schist hills of Central Otago.","ids":["nz-bannockburn","nz-canterbury","nz-central-otago","nz-marlborough","nz-nelson","nz-north-canterbury","nz-south-island","nz-waipara-valley","nz-waitaki-valley-north-otago"]},{"name":"National and multi-region","slug":"national-and-multi-region","tagline":"","ids":["nz-new-zealand"]}],"items":{"nz-auckland":{"name":"Auckland","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"North Island","created":"2018-10-29","area_ha":253,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Pinot Gris","summary":"The Auckland GI covers New Zealand's largest city and its surrounding countryside, with only about 250 ha of vines but many of the country's biggest wine companies' headquarters. It was the heart of twentieth-century New Zealand wine, when Dalmatian, Lebanese and English families made fortified and table wine in Henderson, Kumeu and Huapai. It contains three sub-GIs: Matakana to the north, Kumeu to the north-west and Waiheke Island in the Hauraki Gulf.","terroir":"Warm, humid and wet (more than 1,200 mm of rain a year), on heavy clay soils; vineyards favour slopes and free-draining sites.","producers":["Kumeu River","Babich","Stonyridge","Villa Maria","Man O' War"],"contains":["Matakana","Kumeu","Waiheke Island"],"rslug":"north-island"},"nz-bannockburn":{"name":"Bannockburn","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"South Island","subregion":"Central Otago","created":"2022-02-01","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, Pinot Gris","summary":"The most recent New Zealand wine GI, registered in February 2022, covering a small, warm sub-region of Central Otago on the southern bank of the Kawarau River near Cromwell. Gold miners sluiced its hills in the 1860s and 1870s, leaving the 'Cairnmuir' sluicings, an eerie landscape of cliffs and tailings. Stewart Elms planted Felton Road here in 1991 and 1992; with Mount Difficulty, Akarua and Carrick it made Bannockburn's ripe, structured, mineral Pinot Noir the most celebrated in the region.","terroir":"One of the warmest and driest corners of Central Otago, on schist-derived gravels, loess and clays over sandstone, at about 220 to 400 m.","producers":["Felton Road","Mount Difficulty","Akarua","Carrick","Terra Sancta","Domaine Thomson"],"nerd_note":"Felton Road's Block 3 and Block 5 Pinot Noirs come from parcels of the original Elms planting, on deeper loess and on a more exposed terrace respectively.","rslug":"south-island"},"nz-canterbury":{"name":"Canterbury","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"South Island","created":"2018-02-26","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay","summary":"The Canterbury GI covers the broad province around Christchurch, from the Canterbury Plains to Banks Peninsula. French settlers planted vines at Akaroa in 1840, and Lincoln University's research in the 1970s showed the plains could ripen cool-climate grapes. Today almost all quality vineyards are in North Canterbury, a separate GI inside it.","terroir":"Cool, dry and windy, with the nor'wester (a hot, dry foehn wind) a defining feature.","producers":["Pegasus Bay","Giesen","St Helena (historic)"],"contains":["North Canterbury"],"rslug":"south-island"},"nz-central-hawkes-bay":{"name":"Central Hawke's Bay","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"North Island","subregion":"Hawke's Bay","created":"2019-01-28","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris","summary":"The southern, cooler part of Hawke's Bay around Waipukurau and Waipawa, registered as its own GI in 2019. It has only a few vineyards, but its limestone hills and cooler climate suit Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and aromatic whites.","terroir":"Cooler and more continental than the Heretaunga Plains, with limestone and clay soils.","producers":["Lime Rock"],"nerd_note":"Lime Rock Wines takes its name from the limestone outcrops of its vineyard near Waipawa.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-central-otago":{"name":"Central Otago","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"South Island","created":"2018-10-29","area_ha":2099,"styles":["red","white","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir (about 70 to 80 percent), Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Central Otago is often called the world's most southerly commercial wine region, at around 45 degrees south (vineyards near Sarmiento in Argentine Patagonia, at about 45.6 degrees, now contest the title), and its Pinot Noir is New Zealand's most famous red. It is a dramatic place: dry brown hills of schist, rivers and lakes, gold-rush ruins and snow-capped mountains. Jean Désiré Feraud planted vines at Clyde in 1864, but the modern industry began in the 1970s and 1980s with Rolfe and Lois Mills at Rippon on Lake Wanaka (1975) and Alan Brady at Gibbston Valley (first commercial wine in 1987). It now has about 2,100 ha and more than 230 vineyards.\n\nThe climate is semi-continental: hot, dry summer days, cold nights and frost in spring and autumn, so vineyards occupy slopes and terraces that drain cold air. Pinot Noir here is vivid and dark-fruited, with spice and fine tannin. Sub-regions differ. Gibbston, in the Kawarau Gorge near Queenstown, is the coolest and highest; Bannockburn (a registered GI since 2022) and the Cromwell Basin (Bendigo, Pisa, Lowburn) are the warmest and give the most powerful wines; Alexandra is dry and continental; Wanaka is lakeside and cool. Felton Road (Bannockburn), Rippon (Wanaka), Burn Cottage, Prophet's Rock (Bendigo) and Two Paddocks are among the leaders.","terroir":"Schist-derived gravels and loess (windblown silt), very low rainfall (often under 400 mm), high sunshine and big temperature swings. Most vineyards are irrigated.","producers":["Felton Road","Rippon","Burn Cottage","Prophet's Rock","Two Paddocks","Gibbston Valley","Chard Farm","Mount Difficulty","Quartz Reef","Valli","Mount Edward","Peregrine","Amisfield"],"nerd_note":"Rippon's vineyard on the shore of Lake Wanaka is one of the most photographed in the world; Nick Mills farms it biodynamically.","contains":["Bannockburn","Gibbston (informal)","Bendigo (informal)","Cromwell Basin (informal)","Pisa (informal)","Lowburn (informal)","Alexandra (informal)","Wanaka (informal)"],"rslug":"south-island"},"nz-gisborne":{"name":"Gisborne","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"North Island","created":"2018-02-26","area_ha":1325,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Sauvignon Blanc, Merlot, Gewürztraminer","summary":"Gisborne, on the East Cape, is one of the first places in the world to see the sun each day and calls itself New Zealand's Chardonnay capital. Its fertile alluvial plains grew much of the country's bulk white in the 1970s and 1980s; today its 1,300 ha supply Chardonnay, Pinot Gris and sparkling base, and a few artisans prove its quality. James and Annie Millton started New Zealand's first certified organic and later biodynamic estate here in 1984; their Clos de Ste Anne Chenin Blanc and Chardonnay are reference wines.","terroir":"Warm, sunny and relatively humid; fertile silty loams on the Poverty Bay flats, with clay-limestone hillsides at Manutuke.","producers":["Millton","Spade Oak","Matawhero","TW Wines"],"nerd_note":"Matawhero's Gewürztraminer in the 1970s and 1980s, made by Denis Irwin, was one of the first New Zealand wines to win international praise.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-gladstone":{"name":"Gladstone","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"North Island","subregion":"Wairarapa","created":"2019-01-03","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Riesling","summary":"A tiny rural district in the Ruamahanga River valley between Martinborough and Masterton, registered in 2019. A handful of family vineyards make Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris and Pinot Noir on gravelly river terraces.","terroir":"Alluvial gravels and silts on terraces of the Ruamahanga River; slightly cooler and wetter than Martinborough.","producers":["Gladstone Vineyard","Urlar"],"nerd_note":"Urlar is one of the few Wairarapa estates with organic and biodynamic certification.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-hawkes-bay":{"name":"Hawke's Bay","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"North Island","created":"2018-02-26","area_ha":4454,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Merlot, Chardonnay, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris","summary":"Hawke's Bay is New Zealand's second-largest wine region (about 4,450 ha of producing vines in 2025) and its oldest continuously producing one: French Marist missionaries planted vines in 1851 and their Mission Estate still makes wine at Taradale. It is the country's red-wine heartland, making Bordeaux-style blends of Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc and, increasingly, peppery Syrah, as well as rich Chardonnay.\n\nThe key to its reds is gravel. The Gimblett Gravels, about 800 ha of stony former riverbed west of Hastings, were exposed when a great flood in 1867 diverted the Ngaruroro River. Once considered worthless (a concrete company wanted to quarry them), they absorb daytime heat and drain freely, ripening Syrah and Cabernet in a cool climate; producers formed the Gimblett Gravels Winegrowers Association and trademarked the name in 2001. The Bridge Pa Triangle to the south, on red metal soils, and the limestone hills around Havelock North (home of Te Mata Estate, founded in 1896) are the other key districts. Te Mata's Coleraine blend, first made in 1982, is New Zealand's most famous red.","terroir":"Sunny and dry for the North Island, sheltered from westerlies by the Ruahine and Kaweka ranges. Soils range from free-draining alluvial gravels (Gimblett Gravels, Bridge Pa) to silt loams and limestone hills.","producers":["Te Mata Estate","Craggy Range","Trinity Hill","Stonecroft","Mission Estate","Church Road","Elephant Hill","Clearview","Sileni","Newton Forrest","Vidal","Te Awanga","Smith & Sheth"],"nerd_note":"Alan Limmer planted Syrah at Stonecroft in the Gimblett Gravels area in 1984 and released its first Syrah from the 1989 vintage, the first modern New Zealand Syrah on the market.","contains":["Central Hawke's Bay","Gimblett Gravels (trademark, not a GI)","Bridge Pa Triangle (informal)"],"rslug":"north-island"},"nz-kumeu":{"name":"Kumeu","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"North Island","subregion":"Auckland","created":"2018-10-29","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Merlot","summary":"Kumeu, with neighbouring Huapai and Waimauku north-west of Auckland, is where Dalmatian families such as the Brajkoviches (Kumeu River), Nobilos and Selaks built wineries from the 1930s and 1940s. Its fame today rests on Chardonnay: Kumeu River, run by Michael Brajkovich (New Zealand's first Master of Wine, 1989) and his family, makes some of the finest Chardonnays in the New World, especially the single-vineyard Maté's and Hunting Hill.","terroir":"Heavy clay soils over sandstone that retain water in a wet climate; humid summers favour early-ripening varieties and careful canopy work.","producers":["Kumeu River","Nobilo (historic)","Matua (historic)","Soljans"],"nerd_note":"Kumeu River's Maté's Vineyard, planted in 1990, is named after Maté Brajkovich, who arrived from Dalmatia and bought the property in 1944.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-marlborough":{"name":"Marlborough","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"South Island","created":"2018-02-26","area_ha":32042,"styles":["white","red","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc (about 80 percent), Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"Marlborough, at the north-eastern tip of the South Island, has about 32,000 ha of vines, 73 percent of New Zealand's vineyard, and more than 1,100 vineyards. Sheep grazed its flat Wairau Valley until 1973, when Montana planted vines at Brancott against advice that the region was too cold and dry. Its first Sauvignon Blanc, from the 1979 vintage, showed something new: intensely aromatic wine, with passion fruit, gooseberry and fresh-cut grass, and searing acidity. Hunter's success in London in 1986 and Cloudy Bay's launch in 1985 brought world attention, and Marlborough Sauvignon became one of the most successful wine styles of the modern era.\n\nThe region has three main sub-areas. The Wairau Valley, along the Wairau River, has stony, free-draining alluvial soils on its northern side (Rapaura) that give the classic tropical style. The Southern Valleys (Brancott, Omaka, Fairhall, Ben Morven, Waihopai), on clay-rich soils, give more textured Sauvignon, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The Awatere Valley to the south-east, higher, windier and cooler, gives a leaner, more herbaceous and saline style. Producers such as Dog Point, Greywacke, Clos Henri, Te Whare Ra, Seresin and Churton show that Marlborough can also make complex, barrel-fermented and wild-yeast Sauvignon, serious Pinot Noir and traditional-method sparkling. The flip side is heavy reliance on one grape: the region produced a record crop in 2025 and growers face pressure to diversify.","terroir":"Very sunny (about 2,400 hours a year), dry (about 650 mm of rain) and with big swings between warm days and cool nights. Soils are young alluvial gravels, silts and clays laid down by the Wairau, Awatere and their tributaries.","producers":["Cloudy Bay","Brancott Estate","Dog Point","Greywacke","Clos Henri","Seresin","Te Whare Ra","Hunter's","Fromm","Churton","Yealands","Saint Clair","Villa Maria","Astrolabe","Nautilus"],"nerd_note":"Ivan Sutherland and James Healy, who left Cloudy Bay to found Dog Point, make Section 94, a barrel-fermented Sauvignon Blanc named after a land-survey section.","contains":["Wairau Valley (informal)","Southern Valleys (informal)","Awatere Valley (informal)"],"rslug":"south-island"},"nz-martinborough":{"name":"Martinborough","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"North Island","subregion":"Wairarapa","created":"2018-02-26","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"A small village laid out in the shape of a Union Jack in the 1870s, now one of New Zealand's most important Pinot Noir districts. In 1979 a report by soil scientist Dr Derek Milne compared its climate and free-draining terrace soils to Burgundy; Neil McCallum (Dry River) and Clive Paton (Ata Rangi) planted that year and the next, followed by Martinborough Vineyard and Palliser. Their Pinots, savoury, firm and long-lived, set the standard for New Zealand red in the 1990s. Ata Rangi's Pinot Noir, from the 'Abel' clone said to have been smuggled in from Burgundy, is the region's icon.","terroir":"The Martinborough Terrace, an old river terrace of silt over deep, free-draining gravel; dry, windy and cool, with frost risk. The Te Muna Road area to the south-east has similar soils.","producers":["Ata Rangi","Dry River","Martinborough Vineyard","Escarpment","Palliser","Kusuda","Schubert","Te Kairanga"],"nerd_note":"The 'Abel' or 'Gumboot' clone of Pinot Noir is said to have been confiscated from a traveller's gumboot at Auckland airport by customs officer Malcolm Abel, who propagated it.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-matakana":{"name":"Matakana","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"North Island","subregion":"Auckland","created":"2018-02-26","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Gris, Syrah, Merlot, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc","summary":"A small district about an hour north of Auckland, around the village of Matakana and the Mahurangi coast. The first modern vineyards were planted in the 1960s and 1970s, and today about a dozen boutique producers make Bordeaux blends, Syrah, Pinot Gris and Chardonnay for the weekend visitors from the city.","terroir":"Clay soils over sandstone on rolling hills, with warm, humid summers moderated by sea breezes from both coasts.","producers":["Providence (historic)","Brick Bay","Ascension"],"nerd_note":"Matakana's vineyards share the district with olive groves and a Saturday farmers' market that draws Aucklanders north.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-nelson":{"name":"Nelson","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"South Island","created":"2018-06-26","area_ha":970,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Riesling","summary":"Nelson, at the top of the South Island, is one of the sunniest places in New Zealand and a small, artistic wine region of about 970 ha. It has two distinct areas: the Waimea Plains, with stony alluvial soils that give fresh Sauvignon and aromatic whites, and the Moutere Hills, where clay-bound gravels give richer Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. German settlers planted vines in the 1840s; the modern era began with Hermann Seifried (1973) and Tim and Judy Finn at Neudorf (1978), whose Moutere Chardonnay is one of the country's best.","terroir":"Sheltered by mountains on three sides; high sunshine and moderate rain. Moutere gravel-clays (weathered, iron-rich) contrast with the stony Waimea Plains.","producers":["Neudorf","Seifried","Kina Beach","Greenhough","Rimu Grove"],"nerd_note":"Neudorf's Moutere Chardonnay comes from vines on the clay-gravel 'Moutere Gravels', which hold water and give a dense, mineral wine.","rslug":"south-island"},"nz-new-zealand":{"name":"New Zealand","cat":"GI","subcat":"Enduring GI","region":"National and multi-region","created":"2017-07-27","styles":["white","red","sparkling","rose","sweet"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"The national GI, created by statute as an 'enduring' GI and registered in July 2017. It covers any wine of which at least 85 percent comes from New Zealand, typically blends across regions.","contains":["North Island","South Island"],"rslug":"national-and-multi-region"},"nz-north-canterbury":{"name":"North Canterbury","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"South Island","subregion":"Canterbury","created":"2020-06-29","area_ha":1539,"styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"North Canterbury, registered in 2020, covers the hills north of Christchurch, including the Waipara Valley and the limestone country around Waikari. With about 1,540 ha, it is a source of some of New Zealand's most intellectually interesting wines: Pyramid Valley's and Bell Hill's limestone-grown Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, and Pegasus Bay's Riesling and Pinot.","terroir":"Warm, dry summers and long autumns protected from easterly sea breezes by the Teviotdale Hills; limestone, clay and gravels.","producers":["Pegasus Bay","Pyramid Valley","Bell Hill","Black Estate","Greystone","Muddy Water","Mountford"],"nerd_note":"The limestone at Waikari and Weka Pass is rare in New Zealand, which is one reason Burgundy-obsessed growers chose it.","contains":["Waipara Valley"],"rslug":"south-island"},"nz-north-island":{"name":"North Island","cat":"GI","subcat":"Enduring GI","region":"North Island","created":"2017-07-27","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Merlot, Syrah, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"An enduring GI covering the North Island, used for blends of North Island regions (for example Hawke's Bay and Gisborne fruit). The North Island had about 7,500 ha of vines in 2025, about 17 percent of the national total, led by Hawke's Bay and Gisborne.","contains":["Northland","Auckland","Gisborne","Hawke's Bay","Wairarapa"],"rslug":"north-island"},"nz-northland":{"name":"Northland","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"North Island","created":"2018-02-26","area_ha":72,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Syrah, Pinot Gris, Chambourcin","summary":"New Zealand's northernmost and warmest wine region, and its first: Samuel Marsden planted vines at Kerikeri in 1819 and James Busby made wine at Waitangi in the 1830s. Today about 70 ha of vines support small producers making Syrah, Chardonnay and Pinot Gris in a subtropical, humid climate where no point is more than 50 km from the sea.","terroir":"Warm, humid and wet, with clay soils; disease pressure is the main challenge.","producers":["Marsden Estate","Omata Estate","Karikari Estate"],"nerd_note":"Karikari Estate on the Karikari Peninsula is one of the northernmost vineyards in the country.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-south-island":{"name":"South Island","cat":"GI","subcat":"Enduring GI","region":"South Island","created":"2017-07-27","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris","summary":"An enduring GI covering the South Island, used for blends such as Marlborough and Nelson or Central Otago and Waitaki fruit. The South Island had about 36,700 ha of vines in 2025, about 83 percent of the national total, most of them in Marlborough.","contains":["Nelson","Marlborough","Canterbury","Waitaki Valley North Otago","Central Otago"],"rslug":"south-island"},"nz-waiheke-island":{"name":"Waiheke Island","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"North Island","subregion":"Auckland","created":"2018-02-26","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc","summary":"A hilly island in the Hauraki Gulf, a 40-minute ferry ride from downtown Auckland, which since the 1980s has become a source of expensive Bordeaux blends and Syrah. Goldwater Estate planted the first modern vineyard in 1978 and Stephen White's Stonyridge (1981) made its Larose blend a cult wine. The island is warmer and drier than mainland Auckland, and now has about 30 wineries alongside its holiday homes.","terroir":"Clay over weathered greywacke on steep slopes; warm and maritime, with lower rainfall than Auckland.","producers":["Stonyridge","Man O' War","Destiny Bay","Te Motu","Goldwater (historic)","Obsidian"],"nerd_note":"Destiny Bay sells its Bordeaux blends at prices among the highest in New Zealand.","rslug":"north-island"},"nz-waipara-valley":{"name":"Waipara Valley","cat":"GI","subcat":"Sub-GI","region":"South Island","subregion":"North Canterbury","created":"2018-12-03","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay","summary":"A small, warm valley about an hour north of Christchurch, registered as a GI in 2018 and sitting within North Canterbury. Pegasus Bay, founded by the Donaldson family in 1985, made its name for Riesling (in dry to botrytised styles) and Pinot Noir; Black Estate, Greystone and Mountford followed. The valley is sheltered from cool easterlies by the Teviotdale Hills, so it ripens grapes well for its latitude.","terroir":"Glacial gravels and clay loams on the valley floor, with limestone-rich clays on the slopes; hot dry summers and the nor'wester wind.","producers":["Pegasus Bay","Black Estate","Greystone","Mountford","Muddy Water"],"rslug":"south-island"},"nz-wairarapa":{"name":"Wairarapa","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"North Island","created":"2018-02-26","area_ha":1189,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Syrah","summary":"The Wairarapa lies an hour north-east of Wellington across the Remutaka Range and holds about 1,190 ha of vines, mostly Pinot Noir and Sauvignon Blanc. It contains two registered sub-GIs, Martinborough and Gladstone, and the area around Masterton (Opaki). Boutique producers dominate; the region makes only about 3 percent of New Zealand's wine but a much larger share of its fine Pinot.","terroir":"A rain shadow behind the Remutaka and Tararua ranges, with strong north-westerly winds that cut yields and keep disease low. Free-draining river-terrace soils over gravel.","producers":["Ata Rangi","Dry River","Escarpment","Palliser","Craggy Range (Te Muna)","Kusuda"],"contains":["Martinborough","Gladstone"],"rslug":"north-island"},"nz-waitaki-valley-north-otago":{"name":"Waitaki Valley North Otago","cat":"GI","subcat":"Region","region":"South Island","created":"2018-12-03","area_ha":49,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Riesling, Chardonnay","summary":"One of New Zealand's newest and most marginal regions, in the Waitaki River valley around Kurow and Duntroon, registered in 2018. Its first vines went in around 2001. Limestone outcrops and a cold, frost-prone climate attracted growers such as Jim Jerram (Ostler) and Michelle Richardson. With fewer than 50 ha, it makes taut Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris and Riesling.","terroir":"Limestone and schist gravels with windblown loess; very cool, with a short season and frost risk.","producers":["Ostler","Valli (Waitaki vineyard)","Pasquale"],"rslug":"south-island"}}};
